          "content": "The rain stopped just before the library opened, leaving the steps dark and glossy. Inside, someone had placed a bowl beneath a leak in the ceiling. Each drop struck the water with a small, patient note. It was not quite music, but it gave the morning a rhythm.\n\nAt the returns desk, a boy brought back an atlas older than he was. A folded scrap of paper fell from its pages: a list of places he hoped to see, written in careful capitals. The librarian handed it back without reading past the first line. Some maps, she knew, belonged to their makers.\n\nAcross the room, a woman opened a book of recipes and smiled at a stain shaped like a crescent moon. Perhaps it reminded her of flour on a kitchen table, or of someone who used to cook beside her. No one asked. The library made space for stories people carried but did not tell.\n\nBy noon, sunlight reached the windows. The bowl was full, and the leak had stopped. The librarian emptied it into a plant whose leaves tilted toward the light. She set the bowl back anyway. Weather changed; roofs remembered their weaknesses.\n\nOutside, buses hissed along the curb, and the city resumed its arguments. Inside, pages turned, chairs scraped, and the boy traced a route across the atlas with one finger. He did not know when he would leave. For now, knowing the names of distant places was a way to begin on a quiet morning.",
